1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 13, configuration management with Ansible.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Configuration management means describing the desired state of a server in files, so that any server can be set up the same way, automatically and repeatably, instead of by hand.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Ansible does this over SSH, with no agent on the server.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
First file: the inventory.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
It is the list of servers, grouped by role.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Ours has one group called web, with one server.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,000
This is the template for a real server, and below it the inventory of the lab, where I hide the lab only password.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Variables make the playbook configurable without changing the tasks.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Here are the real ones: the folder on the server, the environment, the public port, and the release being deployed, which is read from the VERSION file of the repository.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
The commit comes from Git, so every deployment can be traced.

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
And the secret key is generated once, stored only on the control machine and in a git ignored file, and never printed.

12
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The playbook is the list of tasks, each with a name.

13
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
It runs on the web group with sudo.

14
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:50,000
In order: install Docker and the Compose plugin, make sure Docker runs and starts on boot, create the application folders, copy the source and Docker files, remember the previous release for rollback, write the environment file, check whether the images already exist, build them if not, start the containers and wait until healthy, then check the portal through Nginx, and check that the running version is the expected one.

15
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Here is one task in detail.

16
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:07,000
It writes the environment file that Docker Compose reads: the secret key, the environment, the port, the version, the commit and the image tag.

17
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The file is owned by root with mode zero six zero zero, so only root can read the secret.

18
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
And no log is set, so Ansible never prints the secret in its output.

19
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Ansible describes the state, and the copy module only changes the file if its content is different.

20
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
This is the idempotency design.

21
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Each task checks the actual state.

22
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Docker is installed?

23
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Then nothing to do.

24
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
The file has this content?

25
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Then nothing to do.

26
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:51,000
For the images, the playbook asks the server which commit the existing image was built from, and builds only if the release and commit are not there yet.

27
00:02:51,000 --> 00:03:01,000
A rebuild would create a new image id and restart the containers every time, so this check is what keeps a second run at zero changes.

28
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Ansible runs from a controller container, because it does not run natively on Windows.

29
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:18,000
First a syntax check, which reads the playbook and the inventory and reports errors without touching any server.

30
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
The answer is just the playbook name: the syntax is valid.

31
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Now the target.

32
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
I start the lab server.

33
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
To be completely clear about what this is: it is not a separate virtual machine.

34
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:43,000
It is a privileged Docker container running Ubuntu twenty four oh four, with systemd and SSH, standing in for a server.

35
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
I print its operating system, and the privileged flag, to prove it.

36
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:57,000
The playbook uses only normal Ubuntu packages and SSH, so on a real Ubuntu server it should behave the same, but that was not tested.

37
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Before the real run, a problem I found while preparing this video, and I want to show it openly.

38
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
This laptop's clock is wrong.

39
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,000
A real web server says the time in G M T is seventeen forty one.

40
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
This PC says twelve eleven in U T C, five and a half hours behind.

41
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Ubuntu's package lists are dated with the real time, so on this fresh server apt refuses them as coming from the future, and the first task of the playbook, installing Docker, would fail.

42
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
I did not change the PC's clock.

43
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:55,000
For this disposable lab only, I switch off apt's date check with one small file, and apt update works.

44
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:03,000
On your own computer, the proper fix is to set the time zone and synchronise the clock in Windows.

45
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
The ping module is not a network ping.

46
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:21,000
It logs in over SSH and runs a small Python check, so a pong proves that the inventory, the SSH login and Python on the server all work.

47
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Now the real run, live.

48
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:38,000
This lab server is brand new, so this is a first provisioning, and it takes a few minutes: it has to install Docker, copy the files and build the images.

49
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Each task prints ok, if the server already matched, or changed, if Ansible had to change something.

50
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,000
On a fresh server I expect most of them to say changed.

51
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Watch the task names scroll by: Docker, the folders, the files, the environment file, the images, the containers, the health check.

52
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:08,000
At the end, the play recap summarises: how many tasks were ok, how many changed, and that none failed.

53
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:09,000
Let me look at the result on the server itself.

54
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
The application folder in slash opt, with the source and the Docker files.

55
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:20,000
The environment file, without the secret, and its permissions: six hundred, owned by root.

56
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:32,000
The containers running inside the server, and finally the portal itself answering on port eighty eighty eight of my computer, with the version and commit it was deployed with.


